## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
- Time/space trade-offs and modular arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for counting shortest paths
function countShortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const dist = dijkstra(graph, s);
  const dag = buildShortestPathDag(graph, dist);
  const order = topologicalSort(dag);
  const ways = new Array(graph.n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) {
    for (const [v] of dag.adj[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple target nodes efficiently?
- How does the solution change if edges can have zero weight, or if counts exceed 64-bit, and you need a different modulus or exact arithmetic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses dynamic programming fundamentals through practical string segmentation, testing understanding of both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic Programming: memoization vs. tabulation
- String matching optimization (startsWith, trie)
- Time and space trade-offs between DP approaches
- State definition and transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function minWordSegmentsTopDown(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
  memo[n] = 0;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    let minSegs = Infinity;
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        const result = dfs(i + word.length);
        if (result !== -1) {
          minSegs = Math.min(minSegs, 1 + result);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[i] = minSegs === Infinity ? -1 : minSegs;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulated solution
function minWordSegmentsBottomUp(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[n] = 0;

  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        if (dp[i + word.length] !== Infinity) {
          dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], 1 + dp[i + word.length]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[0];
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time:** O(n × m × L) where n = |s|, m = dict size, L = avg word length. Using a Trie reduces this to O(n × L).
- **Space:** O(n) for the memo/DP array.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests algorithmic depth and practical handling of worst-case quicksort behavior.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth guard
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid pathological inputs
- In-place partition and recursion depth management
- Complexity: O(n log n) average and worst-case; O(log n) stack space

## Code Example

```javascript
// Introsort skeleton in JS
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  if (n <= 1) return;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n));
  function swap(i, j) { const t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = t; }
  function medianOfThree(lo, mid, hi) {
    if (arr[lo] > arr[mid]) swap(lo, mid);
    if (arr[lo] > arr[hi]) swap(lo, hi);
    if (arr[mid] > arr[hi]) swap(mid, hi);
    return mid;
  }
  function partition(lo, hi) {
    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
    const m = medianOfThree(lo, mid, hi);
    swap(m, hi);
    const pivot = arr[hi];
    let i = lo;
    for (let j = lo; j < hi; j++) {
      if (arr[j] <= pivot) { swap(i, j); i++; }
    }
    swap(i, hi);
    return i;
  }
  function heapSort(lo, hi) {
    const n = hi - lo + 1;
    function heapify(i, size) {
      while (true) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1;
        const r = l + 1;
        let largest = i;
        if (l < size && arr[lo + l] > arr[lo + largest]) largest = l;
        if (r < size && arr[lo + r] > arr[lo + largest]) largest = r;
        if (largest !== i) { swap(lo + i, lo + largest); i = largest; } else break;
      }
    }
    for (let i = Math.floor(n / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) heapify(i, n);
    for (let end = n - 1; end > 0; end--) {
      swap(lo, lo + end);
      heapify(0, end);
    }
  }
  function insertionSort(lo, hi) {
    for (let i = lo + 1; i <= hi; i++) {
      const key = arr[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= lo && arr[j] > key) { arr[j + 1] = arr[j]; j--; }
      arr[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function quickSort(lo, hi, depth) {
    const size = hi - lo + 1;
    if (size <= 16) { insertionSort(lo, hi); return; }
    if (depth <= 0) { heapSort(lo, hi); return; }
    const p = partition(lo, hi);
    quickSort(lo, p - 1, depth - 1);
    quickSort(p + 1, hi, depth - 1);
  }
  quickSort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to sort objects by a numeric key while preserving comparator locality?
- Compare cache behavior of introsort vs pure mergesort on large datasets.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multiple shortest paths and second-best paths with counting, using a single Dijkstra variant. It blends graph traversal and path combinatorics under constraints typical at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Two-best paths per node; counting paths for each distance tier
- Dijkstra relaxation with tie-handling
- Modulo counting for combinatorial paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Two-best Dijkstra (conceptual)
function secondShortest(graph, s, t, MOD){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist1 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const dist2 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const cnt1 = Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt2 = Array(n).fill(0);
  dist1[s] = 0; cnt1[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap();
  pq.push([0, s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist2[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of graph[u]){
      const nd = d + w;
      if (nd < dist1[v]){
        dist2[v] = dist1[v]; cnt2[v] = cnt1[v];
        dist1[v] = nd; cnt1[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist1[v]){
        cnt1[v] = (cnt1[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      } else if (nd > dist1[v] && nd < dist2[v]){
        dist2[v] = nd; cnt2[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist2[v]){
        cnt2[v] = (cnt2[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return [dist2[t], cnt2[t] % MOD];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to count distinct second-shortest paths that share subpaths?  
- How does this adapt if negative weights are allowed but no negative cycles exist?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DP with a multi‑dimensional state and non‑trivial transitions, mixing path constraints with exact move counts. It also probes memoization vs bottom‑up, boundary handling, and memory optimizations essential for large grids.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) representing position and moves
- Transitions from (i-1,j,t-1), (i,j-1,t-1), (i-1,j-1,t-1)
- -INF for invalid states; base cases
- Memory optimization via rolling arrays
- Trade-offs between readability (memoized DFS) and performance (tabulation)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridSum(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array.from({length:m}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  function dfs(i,j,t){
    if(i<0||j<0||t<0) return NEG;
    if(i===0&&j===0&&t===0) return grid[0][0];
    if(memo[i][j][t]!==NEG) return memo[i][j][t];
    const best = Math.max(dfs(i-1,j,t-1), dfs(i,j-1,t-1), dfs(i-1,j-1,t-1));
    memo[i][j][t] = (best===NEG)? NEG : grid[i][j] + best;
    return memo[i][j][t];
  }
  return dfs(n-1,m-1,k);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the path achieving the maximum sum?
- How would this change if some moves were disallowed or weights negative?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical graph DP with a real-world flavor (service startup), ensuring knowledge of cycle detection, topological order, and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn/DFS)
- DAG DP for path/finish times
- Cycle detection via processed node count

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestStartup(graph, startup, t) {
  const V = graph.length;
  const indeg = new Array(V).fill(0);
  for (let u = 0; u < V; u++) for (const v of graph[u]) indeg[v]++;
  const q = [];
  const earliest = startup.slice();
  for (let i = 0; i < V; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let seen = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    seen++;
    for (const v of graph[u]) {
      earliest[v] = Math.max(earliest[v], earliest[u] + startup[v]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (seen !== V) throw new Error('cycle detected');
  return earliest[t];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to dynamic updates in graph or startup times?
- How would you extend to multiple targets efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question blends BFS reachability, Dijkstra shortest paths, and counting shortest paths via a DAG and topological order.

## Key Concepts
- BFS/DFS reachability
- Dijkstra's algorithm
- Shortest-path DAG
- Topological DP and modular counting

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, adj, s) {
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity); dist[s] = 0;
  // priority queue based Dijkstra (pseudo-notation)
  const pq = new MinPQ((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  pq.enqueue([0,s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d,u] = pq.dequeue(); if (d!==dist[u]) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]){
      if (dist[v] > d + w){ dist[v] = d + w; pq.enqueue([dist[v], v]); }
    }
  }
  const dag = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  for (let u=0; u<n; u++) for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]) if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) dag[u].push(v);
  const order = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i).sort((a,b)=>dist[a]-dist[b]);
  const MOD = 1000000007; const ways = Array(n).fill(0); ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) for (const v of dag[u]) ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would zero-weight edges affect the approach? 
- How to extend to count modulo a large prime or handle multiple sources?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem demonstrates how to model a constrained, single-use resource within a shortest-path framework. It tests understanding of state-space expansion and whether a candidate can adapt classic algorithms to modified constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **State-Space Expansion:** Augment each vertex with a binary flag indicating coupon usage
- **Layer 0 (coupon unused):** Can transition to layer 0 (full cost) or layer 1 (half cost)
- **Layer 1 (coupon used):** Can only transition to layer 1 (full cost only)
- **Path Reconstruction:** Track predecessor states to identify which edge receives the discount

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithCoupon(graph, s, t) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [Infinity, Infinity]);
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [null, null]);
  const pq = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => a.dist < b.dist);

  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.push({ u: s, used: 0, dist: 0 });

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const { u, used, dist: d } = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if (u === t) break;

    for (const { v, w } of graph[u]) {
      // Transition without using coupon
      if (d + w < dist[v][used]) {
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = { u, used };
        pq.push({ u: v, used, dist: dist[v][used] });
      }
      // Transition using coupon (only if not yet used)
      if (used === 0) {
        const newDist = d + w / 2;
        if (newDist < dist[v][1]) {
          dist[v][1] = newDist;
          prev[v][1] = { u, used, discounted: { from: u, to: v, weight: w } };
          pq.push({ u: v, used: 1, dist: newDist });
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const best = dist[t][0] < dist[t][1] ? 0 : 1;
  return { distance: dist[t][best], finalState: best };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to k coupons instead of one?
- How would you allow the discount on any one edge (not just along a shortest path)?
- How does this change if the coupon provides a fixed discount rather than percentage reduction?

## Why This Is Asked
Autocompletion is common in editors and apps; this tests a lightweight data structure for fast prefix queries and shows practical space/time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with per-node prefix counts
- O(len(prefix)) lookup
- Space proportional to total characters in dictionary
- Edge cases: empty prefix, missing path

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.count = 0;
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.count++;
    }
  }
  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support deletions of words while maintaining correct counts?
- How would you extend to Unicode or larger alphabets while preserving performance?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests robustness of sorting mindset under memory constraints and heavy duplicates, plus guarantees on worst-case performance.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: quicksort with a depth limit that falls back to heapsort
- Three-way partition: handles many duplicates efficiently
- In-place, cache-friendly data movement and tail recursion optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function partitionDutch(a, lo, hi, pivot){
  let i = lo, lt = lo, gt = hi;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  return [lt, gt];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to be stable?
- How does memory usage change with large datasets?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates dynamic programming skills with state expansion, requiring candidates to track whether the cell removal has been used. It tests proper handling of grid boundary conditions and provides opportunities to discuss space optimization strategies and compare memoization versus tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with auxiliary state tracking
- Space optimization using rolling arrays
- Boundary condition handling
- State transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPathWithOneRemoval(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));

  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i-1][j]);
      }
      if (j > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i][j-1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.min(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
- Time: O(m × n) - we process each cell once
- Space: O(m × n) - can be optimized to O(n) using rolling arrays since we only need the previous row

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicate values are common in real-world data sets. Traditional 2-way quicksort performs poorly with many duplicates, leading to unnecessary comparisons and degraded performance. 3-way partitioning addresses this by grouping equal elements together, significantly reducing the algorithm's work.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag algorithm)**: Separates array into < pivot, == pivot, and > pivot regions
- **Randomized pivot selection**: Prevents worst-case O(n²) scenarios on already sorted or adversarial input
- **In-place sorting**: Operates with O(1) additional space, using only O(log n) expected stack depth
- **Performance optimization**: Reduces comparisons from O(n log n) to O(n) when many duplicates exist

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  // Random pivot selection
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;

  // 3-way partitioning
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++;
      i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++; // a[i] === pivot
    }
  }

  // Recursively sort subarrays
  quicksort3(a, lo, lt - 1);
  quicksort3(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Partitioning Strategy

The algorithm maintains three regions during partitioning:
- `[lo, lt-1]`: elements < pivot
- `[lt, gt]`: elements === pivot (being processed)
- `[gt+1, hi]`: elements > pivot

As we iterate, elements are swapped into their appropriate region, with equal elements naturally forming the middle section that needs no further processing.

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of introsort-style hybrids, balancing average performance with worst-case guarantees in production environments. It tests pivot strategies, partitioning, depth tracking, and in-place constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth-based switch
- Median-of-three pivot: reduces chance of worst-case partitions
- 3-way partitioning: handles duplicates efficiently
- Insertion sort for tiny subarrays: amortized gains

## Code Example

